# REA Sketch

Working local canvas editor: draw rectangles, ellipses, diamonds, plain straight lines, straight arrows, text and freehand strokes. Line endpoints retain their direction when dragged or resized left/up; line painting and SVG do not add arrowheads. Diamonds use a four-edge polygon for painting and hit selection rather than the rectangle's corners. Select and drag shapes to move them. Shift-click or drag empty space to select multiple shapes; **Group**, **Ungroup** and grouped dragging operate on the editable selection. A single selected rectangle, ellipse, diamond, line, arrow or text box has a resize handle. Freehand points cannot yet be resized or individually edited. Stroke/fill/text controls apply to the selection. Undo/redo, local save/load/delete, wheel zoom, Pan or Space+drag, Studio JSON import/export, SVG export and native `.excalidraw` import/export are available.

Keyboard controls work while the canvas is focused: Delete/Backspace removes selection, Ctrl/Cmd+A selects all editable shapes, Ctrl/Cmd+G groups, Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+G ungroups, Ctrl/Cmd+Z undoes and Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z redoes. Escape cancels a gesture or clears selection. Undo/redo retains up to 100 open-editor checkpoints; it is not persisted across editor reloads.

## Native Excalidraw contract

The compatibility target is the studied **`@excalidraw/excalidraw` 0.18.1** release, tag `v0.18.1`, source commit `a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84`. The `.excalidraw` scene envelope is `type: "excalidraw", version: 2` with elements, app state and optional image files. This version number describes the file format, not the npm release.

Native import retains the complete parsed JSON document. An unchanged native export preserves the original values, field ordering, unknown properties, unsupported elements, deleted records, app state, image-file records and custom metadata. Whitespace and numeric lexical spellings are not retained. This is a preservation roundtrip, not a promise that every imported element is rendered. Metadata remains inert JSON: this editor does not execute links/HTML or fetch/decode/render imported images.

Editable projections cover unrotated, unlocked rectangles, ellipses and diamonds, unbound straight two-point lines/arrows, standalone auto-resizing text, and freehand strokes within the editor's limits. The canvas uses plain geometry and a system sans-serif font. Roughness, hatch patterns, opacity, corner styles, native fonts, arrowhead variants and pressure data are retained in native exports but their appearance is approximated in the preview. Native line arrowheads remain preserved with a warning and are not painted. Bound lines/arrows, container text, non-auto-resizing text, rotation, locked objects, multi-point/curved/elbow linear geometry, images, frames and unknown element types are preserved without editable rendering. An always-visible import panel lists unsupported/approximate content and explicitly says the canvas and SVG show editable projections only. Groups containing non-editable records receive an additional warning: group actions affect editable projections only.

Editing a supported projection updates only the changed mapped fields and increments its element version. Geometry-only changes retain native colors and other appearance metadata exactly. Changing text updates both native `text` and `originalText`. Existing freehand pressure metadata survives moves; a point-count change resets unmatched pressures to simulated pressure. Removing an imported editable element exports its original record with `isDeleted: true`; deleting a saved Studio scene removes its local index entry. Unsupported records cannot be replaced by creating a shape with their reserved identifier. Externally bound elements are not recalculated when a supported container moves; this limitation is reported for imported containers with bindings. New shapes export native element fields. A one-point new freehand dot exports two coincident points so the original small-element filter does not discard it.

Native documents are limited to **4 MiB of UTF-8 JSON**, 2,000 element records, 100,000 metadata nodes and nesting depth 30. Editable scenes allow 2,000 shapes, 5,000 text characters or freehand points per shape, 20 group levels, zoom 0.1–5, stroke width 0.1–30, and coordinate magnitude at most 1,000,000. Oversized, non-finite or malformed JSON is rejected. Valid finite geometry outside editable bounds stays preserved and is listed as non-editable. Viewport values may be clamped for the editor, while unchanged export retains original app state. Internal preservation fields in Studio JSON are not a public schema for manually fabricating imported records.

The line/diamond additions are source-assisted from the pinned MIT release and do not import the historical study's reconstruction or vendor runtime. Retained source/unchanged-helper digests and license are in `results/geometry-source-study/stage-source/source-provenance.json.snapshot` and `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICE.md`. Primary pinned specifications: [scene JSON schema](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw/blob/a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84/dev-docs/docs/codebase/json-schema.mdx), [element type definitions](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw/blob/a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84/packages/excalidraw/element/types.ts), [original JSON import/export](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw/blob/a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84/packages/excalidraw/data/json.ts), [element restoration](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw/blob/a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84/packages/excalidraw/data/restore.ts).

## Current gaps

This is **not a complete Excalidraw replacement**. It lacks native rough/pressure rendering, multi-point/curved/polygon tools, rotation, snapping, layer/order controls, bound-line/arrow maintenance, rich typography and native text layout, editable images, libraries, live collaboration, encrypted sharing, cloud storage, PNG/clipboard export, embedded-scene images, full mobile gestures and broad accessibility support. Text-box resizing changes selection geometry without font scaling or wrapping. Groups currently support select/move/delete/style operations and nested outer-group removal; they do not provide complete Excalidraw group-edit mode, mixed opaque-record editing or group resizing. Diamond preview anchors match the original `floor(size/2)+1` helper for normal dimensions; sub-pixel/zero sizes clamp that offset to preview bounds, which is a deliberate limited difference. A negative imported diamond keeps its exact raw geometry until geometry editing, then exports canonical positive dimensions once; style-only edits retain raw dimensions. No feature parity, universal quality improvement or broad real-world file compatibility has been established.

## Validation and exact original oracle

Run the focused collector from the repository root:

```text
node products/rea-studio/scripts/verify-sketch-geometry.mjs
```

The core/API suite covers save/reopen/export using the real disk store, unchanged native preservation, selective native edits, arrow normalization, deletion tombstones, unknown image/metadata retention, safe SVG text/freehand output, bounds, malformed documents, reserved identities, groups and undo. Client tests invoke real editor event handlers against a deterministic DOM/canvas stand-in: freehand drawing, grouped moves, undo/redo, native-file import, persistent warnings and saved native roundtrip. These checks do not replace browser usability or accessibility testing.

The test-only original oracle reads the retained original npm archive and development bundle, verifies their SHA-256 digests, and extracts unchanged released helpers into an isolated VM. The application never evaluates vendor code. Six fully specified exported elements cover rectangle, ellipse, straight arrow, two texts and freehand. Original `restoreElement` accepted their individual element data with matching geometry/points; the original local `serializeAsJSON` output round-tripped through this editor, including a fixture with image files and unknown metadata. The original restoration of an edited imported rectangle also preserves custom metadata. The receipt is [results/excalidraw-compatibility.json](results/excalidraw-compatibility.json), with original digests and extracted-helper hashes. Synthetic fixtures are authored in [fixtures.mjs](fixtures.mjs).

**Oracle limits:** `isValidExcalidrawData` is only a permissive envelope predicate and even accepts malformed element contents. It is never treated as full element validation. Individual restoration and local serialization are exercised in isolation with fully specified data, avoiding ambient default/font/binding dependencies. Full `restoreElements`, app-state restoration, import UI, renderer, ordering repair, legacy migrations, binding repair, font metrics and broad real-world native scenes remain untested. If the retained original archive is unavailable, the oracle test explicitly skips; that is not a compatibility pass. No parity conclusion follows from these tests.

The geometry comparison additionally runs eight signed line configurations and eight diamond anchor/restoration configurations against unchanged pinned release helpers. Seventeen recorded comparative contexts include original local serialization. The focused integration suite has 28 tests, including new canvas handler painting/draw/resize, persistence, native metadata preservation and group/history coverage. Current original observations are `results/geometry-original-observations.json`; `results/excalidraw-compatibility.json` remains historical evidence attributed to the preserved production baseline. Diamond interiors are selectable even with no fill, consistent with this editor's geometric selection rather than a claimed original transparent-shape hit contract. Actual staged browser evidence and the unchanged JPEG capture are retained under `results/geometry-source-study/stage-evidence/` with their original-to-snapshot mapping in the strict manifest. Shared-host browser verification is recorded separately.

Current integration evidence is results/geometry-validation.json. Tests write detailed original observations only to app-owned .local or the collector-selected owned directory, and emit a small stdout count marker. The focused collector publishes results after the source-stable run. Exact 51-source stage, failure, license and browser history is under results/geometry-source-study with a strict manifest; the original production compatibility receipt remains historical. Original archive/bundle/source-map bytes are app-local ignored cache only; bootstrap them with the focused collector, which authenticates the official publisher URL/archive SHA and extracted file SHAs. No original runtime is loaded by the editor.

To prepare only the ignored original oracle cache without running tests or writing published results, use `node products/rea-studio/scripts/verify-sketch-geometry.mjs --bootstrap-oracle`. The official npm publisher URL is `https://registry.npmjs.org/@excalidraw/excalidraw/-/excalidraw-0.18.1.tgz`; its SHA-256 must be `b280d4b364b65cba264c5aa4e7435cc2ce6421eabbbef9765a56997a0fadf534`. Extracted development bundle and source-map hashes are pinned independently in `geometry-oracle.mjs`.

## Collector recovery and resource boundaries

The [collector follow-up](results/collector-followup/integration-validation.json) records 17 passing synthetic process groups against the current collector and process helper, separate from the preserved 28 geometry tests. Child execution defaults to 30 seconds, 4 MiB stdout, 1 MiB stderr and a five-second cleanup interval. Arguments and environment are copied before asynchronous work; only the child test-context marker is removed. Cleanup targets the owned PID and its process tree or group. Diagnostic files use exclusive creation and retain bounded partial output on failure. Before/after source hashing is bounded to explicit names and sizes; an endpoint hash is not continuous filesystem monitoring or atomic publication.

The [independent review](results/collector-followup/independent-review/preservation-manifest.json) reruns these 17 groups and verifies all recorded owned root/grandchild PIDs are absent. This finite result does not guarantee absence of every descendant: if the root process has already exited, tree cleanup cannot establish that an unrecorded orphan exited. Bootstrap, inventory hashing, diagnostic filesystem writes and the trusted in-process legacy oracle remain outside the child execution deadline. The follow-up preserves the exact previous collector, its geometry receipt, staged failures and adapter sources without relabeling the old geometry proof as a new execution. These bounds support reproducible development tests; process isolation and universal reliability remain unestablished.

## Alignment and distribution

Six selection actions align left/center/right or top/middle/bottom. Horizontal and vertical distribution arrange separate shapes or outer groups, retaining their internal offsets. Shift selection expands a selected outer group to its editable members. Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+arrow aligns to the corresponding edge; Alt+H/V distributes horizontally/vertically. Changed arrangements create one undo checkpoint, retain selection, and survive save/reopen and native geometry export. A single group or already arranged selection creates no undo checkpoint. Actions refuse an active drawing gesture. Signed line endpoints and freehand point bounds remain intact. Native groups with preserved non-editable members and containers with external bindings refuse layout because those dependencies cannot yet be maintained. Candidate coordinates retain the existing finite editor bounds.

These actions are source-assisted from the pinned Excalidraw0.18.1 source. Original helper verification is scoped to unrotated, unbound fixtures and does not execute the original renderer, workbench, frame membership or full binding engine. Full native layout, rotation, text metrics and original GUI comparison remain open.
